in 2015, my then 24-year-old son was diagnosed with type 1 diabetes. He lived at home. He worked a good job. He was still under my insurance. And at that point, he was still able to afford his insulin. It was expensive, but it was affordable. Even with insurance, he was running about $300 a month out of pocket every time he went to the pharmacy. And that was for insulin and diabetic supplies. Well, in 2017, when he turned 26, he was no longer eligible to stay on my insurance. And what we found through the marketplace for a replacement insurance policy for him was not affordable. It was going to run about $450 a month with a $7,600 deductible.

